[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,4.2);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (3.6,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:3.0,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.4*\x*\x});
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.6,2.1) {Population};
\node[font=\small] at (1.8,-0.6) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
A population of bacteria in a petri dish is growing. The graph shows the population over time. Describe how the population is changing, using one of the phrases: increasing at a constant rate / increasing at an increasing rate / increasing at a decreasing rate.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3.9);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (3.8,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:3.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{-0.32*(\x-3.2)*(\x-3.2)+3.277});
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.6,1.9) {People outside the gates};
\node[font=\small] at (1.9,-0.6) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
A crowd is gathering outside a stadium before a big match. The graph shows the number of people outside the gates over time. Describe how the crowd size is changing, using one of the phrases: increasing at a constant rate / increasing at an increasing rate / increasing at a decreasing rate.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3.1);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (3.6,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:3.0,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2.7-0.3*\x*\x});
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.6,1.55) {Battery charge};
\node[font=\small] at (1.8,-0.6) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The charge remaining in a laptop battery drops slowly at first, then faster and faster as the battery gets low. The graph shows the battery charge over time. Describe how the charge is changing, using one of the phrases: decreasing at a constant rate / decreasing at an increasing rate / decreasing at a decreasing rate.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3.6);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (3.8,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:3.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{3.2*exp(-0.9*\x)});
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.6,1.8) {Temperature};
\node[font=\small] at (1.9,-0.6) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
A cup of coffee is cooling down. The graph shows its temperature over time. Describe how the temperature is changing, using one of the phrases: decreasing at a constant rate / decreasing at an increasing rate / decreasing at a decreasing rate.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3.4);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (3.8,0);
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0) -- (3.2,3.0);
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.6,1.7) {Taxi fare};
\node[font=\small] at (1.9,-0.6) {Distance travelled};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
A taxi fare is charged at a fixed rate per kilometre travelled. The graph shows the fare as the distance travelled increases. Describe how the fare is changing, using one of the phrases: increasing at a constant rate / increasing at an increasing rate / increasing at a decreasing rate.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3.8);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (4.0,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:3.4,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{-0.28*(\x-3.4)*(\x-3.4)+3.237});
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.6,1.9) {Oven temperature};
\node[font=\small] at (2.0,-0.6) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
A student says this graph shows the oven's temperature increasing at an increasing rate as it heats up. Is the student correct? Explain, and give the correct description if not.

development.question_text:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.95]
\begin{scope}[xshift=0cm]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2.8);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(2.8,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:2.4,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.4*\x*\x});
\node[rotate=90,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.5,1.4) {Population};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.4,-0.5) {Time};
\node[font=\small] at (1.4,-1.1) {Country X};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[xshift=4.4cm]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2.8);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(2.8,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:2.4,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{-0.4*(\x-2.4)*(\x-2.4)+2.304});
\node[rotate=90,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.5,1.4) {Population};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.4,-0.5) {Time};
\node[font=\small] at (1.4,-1.1) {Country Y};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graphs show the populations of two countries over the same period, both starting from a similar population and reaching a similar population. Which country's population growth is speeding up over time, and which is levelling off? Explain using the shape of each graph.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.3]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,1.5);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (5.2,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:1.8,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{-0.32*(\x-1.8)*(\x-1.8)+1.037});
\draw[thick,blue] (1.8,1.037) -- (2.6,1.037);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:2.0,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x+2.6},{1.037*exp(-0.75*\x)});
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.8,0.75) {Temperature};
\node[font=\small] at (2.6,-0.55) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
A metal rod is heated in a furnace and then left to cool. The graph shows its temperature over time, in three stages: heating, then held at the furnace's maximum temperature, then cooling. Describe how the temperature is changing during each of the three stages, using the rate vocabulary where it applies.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.8]
\begin{scope}[xshift=0cm]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2.6);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(2.4,0);
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0) -- (2.0,1.8);
\node[rotate=90,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.5,1.3) {Revenue};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.2,-0.5) {Time};
\node[font=\small] at (1.2,-1.1) {Graph P};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[xshift=3.2cm]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2.6);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(2.4,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:2.0,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.45*\x*\x});
\node[rotate=90,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.5,1.3) {Revenue};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.2,-0.5) {Time};
\node[font=\small] at (1.2,-1.1) {Graph Q};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[xshift=6.4cm]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2.6);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(2.4,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:2.0,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{-0.45*(\x-2.0)*(\x-2.0)+1.8});
\node[rotate=90,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.5,1.3) {Revenue};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.2,-0.5) {Time};
\node[font=\small] at (1.2,-1.1) {Graph R};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Graphs P, Q and R all show a company's revenue increasing over the same period, starting and ending at similar values, but in different ways. Which graph shows revenue increasing at an increasing rate? Explain.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.05]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3.0);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (3.4,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:2.8,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2.666-0.34*\x*\x});
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.6,1.5) {Unsold raffle tickets};
\node[font=\small] at (1.7,-0.6) {Time until the draw};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The number of unsold raffle tickets drops slowly at first, then more and more quickly as the draw time approaches. A student describes this graph as "decreasing at a decreasing rate", reasoning that ticket sales should slow down over time. Explain why the student is not correct, and give the correct description.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.95]
\begin{scope}[xshift=0cm]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(2.8,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:2.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{1.936-0.4*\x*\x});
\node[rotate=90,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.5,1.2) {Charge};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.4,-0.5) {Time};
\node[font=\small] at (1.4,-1.1) {Battery X};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[xshift=4.2cm]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(2.8,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:2.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{1.936*exp(-1.0*\x)});
\node[rotate=90,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.5,1.2) {Charge};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.4,-0.5) {Time};
\node[font=\small] at (1.4,-1.1) {Battery Y};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Two phones are left switched on, and their batteries drain from a similar starting charge. Graph X shows Battery X's charge over time; Graph Y shows Battery Y's. Which battery is losing charge faster and faster, and which is losing charge more and more slowly? Explain using the shape of each graph.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.2]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,1.8);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (5.0,0);
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0) -- (1.6,1.4);
\draw[thick,blue] (1.6,1.4) -- (2.6,1.4);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:1.8,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x+2.6},{1.4*exp(-0.85*\x)});
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.7,0.9) {Water height};
\node[font=\small] at (2.5,-0.5) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graph shows the height of the water in a bathtub over three stages: the tap is turned on at a steady flow, someone lies in the bath, then the plug is pulled and the water drains. Describe how the water height is changing during each of the three stages, using the rate vocabulary where it applies.

mastery.question_text:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.95]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3.0);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (5.8,0);
\draw[thick,blue] (0,2.4) -- (1,2.4);
\draw[thick,blue] (1,2.4) -- (2.4,1.5);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:1.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x+2.4},{1.5-0.55*\x*\x});
\draw[thick,blue] (3.6,0.708) -- (3.6,2.6);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:1.6,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x+3.6},{2.6*exp(-0.55*\x)});
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.7,1.5) {Cans in the machine};
\node[font=\small] at (2.9,-0.6) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graph shows the number of cans of soft drink in a school vending machine across one school day, in five stages: no sales before school, steady sales during morning break, a lunchtime rush, an instant restock, then a quiet afternoon. Describe what happens to the number of cans during each stage, using the rate vocabulary where it applies.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.95]
\begin{scope}[xshift=0cm]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(2.6,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:2.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.413*\x*\x});
\node[rotate=90,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.5,1.2) {Population};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.3,-0.5) {Time};
\node[font=\small] at (1.3,-1.1) {Country A};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[xshift=4.2cm]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(2.6,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:2.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{-0.41*(\x-2.2)*(\x-2.2)+1.984});
\node[rotate=90,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.5,1.2) {Population};
\node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.3,-0.5) {Time};
\node[font=\small] at (1.3,-1.1) {Country B};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graphs show the populations of Country A and Country B over the same 20-year period, both starting at the same population and reaching a similar population by the end. Describe the growth of each country's population using the rate vocabulary, and explain which country is likely to have the larger population a few years beyond the graphs shown, if both trends continue.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.05]
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,3.2);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (3.6,0);
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0:3.0,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2.88-0.32*\x*\x});
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.6,1.6) {Reservoir water level};
\node[font=\small] at (1.8,-0.6) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The graph shows the water level in a reservoir during a drought, as it steadily empties. A student describes the graph as "the water level is decreasing at a decreasing rate, because the level keeps dropping". Identify the error in the student's description, and give the correct description.
